## Execution Plan
Follow this plan step by step:
1. Investigate the existing CMS codebase to locate content editing components, content storage models, and any existing preview or draft mechanisms.
2. Identify how locale/market switching currently works for GO, UP, MX markets — examine routing, locale context providers, and content-fetching APIs.
3. Determine the real-time update strategy available in the stack (WebSocket, SSE, polling) by inspecting server configuration and existing real-time features.
4. Design a live preview data flow: content editor changes → backend receives draft update → preview component re-renders with updated content for the selected locale/market.
5. Implement a backend endpoint or WebSocket handler that accepts draft content changes and broadcasts them to connected preview clients, including locale and market identifiers.
6. Implement a frontend live preview component or route that subscribes to draft content updates and renders the content in real time, respecting the selected GO/UP/MX locale.
7. Wire the preview component into the CMS editor UI — add a "Preview" button or panel that opens the live preview, passing the current content draft and locale context.
8. Add locale/market switching controls within the preview so editors can toggle between GO, UP, MX and see locale-specific content rendering live.
9. Write unit tests for the backend draft-broadcast logic (message format, locale routing, connection handling) and frontend tests for the preview component (subscription, re-render on update, locale switching).
10. Run the full test suite and manually verify live preview works across all three markets (GO, UP, MX) with concurrent edits.
